          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) is persistent, excessive worry about many everyday matters. It is more than ordinary worry because of how long it lasts, how broad it is, and how much it gets in the way of daily life. (Source: *Generalized anxiety disorder*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**How it differs from ordinary worry**\n\n- **Duration:** The worry occurs more days than not, for at least six months.\n- **Breadth:** The worry moves from topic to topic, such as work, health, money and family, rather than fixing on one situation.\n- **Control:** The person finds it hard to stop or control the worry.\n- **Proportion:** The worry is out of proportion to the actual circumstances.\n- **Persistence:** It continues even when the circumstances change.\n- **Impairment:** It causes real difficulty with functioning.\n\nAnxiety itself is a normal response to threat. Ordinary worry is usually tied to a specific problem and fades once the problem is dealt with.\n\nThe worry often comes with restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. (Source: same reference.)\n\n**Classification**\n\nThe ICD-10-CM chapter on mental and behavioral disorders places generalized anxiety disorder among the anxiety and stress-related disorders, in the F40 to F48 range. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 5*.) The retrieved text did not give the exact code for GAD, so I cannot confirm it here. Please check the code book or a coder for that.\n\n**What the sources do not cover**\n\nThe retrieved material does not give the full diagnostic checklist or the exact symptom counts, so this is an outline and not a set of diagnostic criteria. Whether someone meets the criteria is a question for a clinician. A screening questionnaire (the seven-item GAD-7) exists, but screening is not a diagnosis. (Source: *Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care*.)\n\nIf worry is persistent and affecting your work, sleep or relationships, a doctor or mental health professional is a good person to talk to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
